17:["$","main",null,{"id":"top","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface/40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10 sm:px-8","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"/operating-system","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-mute transition hover:text-accent","children":"← All chapters"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent/12 px-2.5 py-0.5","children":"Chapter 26"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-mute","children":["· ",17," min read · illustrated"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-hand text-5xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-6xl","children":"I/O Hardware & Software Layers"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-lg text-mute","children":"How one read() call reaches a disk, a keyboard or a network card, and stays the same shape for all of them"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-3","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"So far this course has been mostly about the CPU and memory: how a process runs, how threads are scheduled, how a virtual address becomes a physical frame. But a computer that could only compute would be useless. It has to talk to the outside world, read files, draw pixels, send packets, listen to a keyboard, spin a disk. That is input/output, and it is where the tidy world of registers and page tables meets a chaotic zoo of physical devices, each with its own quirks, speeds and failure modes."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"This chapter opens Part E, on storage, I/O and file systems. Before we can talk about disks and file systems in the chapters ahead, we need the machinery that moves bytes between a program and any device at all. We will build it from the bottom up: the device controllers and registers the hardware exposes, the three ways the CPU can wait for a device (polling, interrupts and DMA), and then the layered stack of OS software that turns all of that into a single, uniform read() and write() your code can call without caring what is on the other end."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"The payoff is one of the most quietly powerful ideas in systems: the same handful of calls work whether the bytes come from an SSD, a pipe, a terminal or a socket. We lean on the hardware from Chapter 3, interrupts, buses, DMA, memory-mapped registers, and now watch the OS put it to work. Throughout, the engineering angle: why buffered writes can vanish in a crash, why a syscall per byte is a performance sin, and why \"everything is a file\" is more than a slogan."}]]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 py-12 sm:px-8","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"sticky top-8 hidden h-fit w-56 shrink-0 lg:block","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-hand text-xl font-bold text-ink","children":"On this page"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-why-io-is-hard",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-why-io-is-hard","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Why I/O is genuinely hard"}]]}]}],["$","li","02-controllers-and-registers",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#02-controllers-and-registers","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":["$L21","$L22"]}]}],"$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26","$L27","$L28"]}]]}],"$L29"]}],"$L2a"]}]
